{% extends "_base.j2" %}
{% block main %}
<span class="eyebrow">Learn · evidence lifecycle</span><h1>Testcase lifecycle</h1><p class="page-hero">DWARF has retained <strong>{{ testcase_count }} testcase record{{ '' if testcase_count == 1 else 's' }}</strong> in <strong>{{ bucket_count }} bucket{{ '' if bucket_count == 1 else 's' }}</strong>. The Operate pages are read-only views of the same state used by the existing CLI replay, minimization, comparison, and promotion workflow.</p>
<div class="definition-actions"><a class="cta" href="/operate/testcases">Browse testcases</a><a class="cta cta--ghost" href="/operate/testcase-buckets">Browse buckets</a><a class="cta cta--ghost" href="/learn/cli#cli-testcase">CLI reference</a></div><hr class="hairline">
<section><span class="eyebrow">Terms</span><h2>What each retained object means</h2><dl class="definition-summary testcase-term-grid"><div><dt>Testcase</dt><dd>A persisted lifecycle record for one interesting input or runtime anomaly.</dd></div><div><dt>Crash</dt><dd>A testcase classification indicating abnormal target termination; it is evidence to triage, not automatically a vulnerability.</dd></div><div><dt>Finding</dt><dd>A bucket promotion state applied only after review supports treating the grouped behavior as a confirmed issue.</dd></div><div><dt>Bucket</dt><dd>A derived group whose cases share source, replay, comparison, behavior, and resource signatures.</dd></div><div><dt>Corpus input</dt><dd>Bytes retained to seed future fuzzing; promotion into a corpus is distinct from classifying a testcase.</dd></div><div><dt>Bundle</dt><dd>A portable run evidence package that may contain testcase lifecycle outputs alongside manifests, logs, and attestations.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section><span class="eyebrow">Flow</span><h2>Existing lifecycle, end to end</h2><ol><li>A fuzz or real-node scenario retains an interesting input or runtime anomaly with its source run and provenance.</li><li>The lifecycle assigns a stable testcase id and bucket signature, then records replay and compare queue state.</li><li>Exact retained bytes may be replayed against Amaru, Cardano-node, or both through the normal scenario executor.</li><li>Reproducible inputs may be minimized; differential runs retain outcomes plus behavior and resource signatures.</li><li>Reviewers promote the entire evidence-equivalent bucket to candidate, validated, or finding through the CLI.</li><li>Separate corpus management may retain useful seeds even when the behavior is clean or not a security finding.</li></ol></section>
<section><span class="eyebrow">Boundaries</span><h2>What the catalog does not claim</h2><p>A recorded crash is not automatically a product vulnerability. A bucket is a reproducible grouping, not a severity score. Missing artifacts and malformed records remain visible as diagnostics. Viewing either catalog does not replay, minimize, compare, promote, delete, or repair state; those mutations remain explicit CLI operations.</p></section>
